    icon: '💡',
  },
  data: {
    LLM: "Early Game: Prioritize revealing your face-down cards quickly by drawing from the stock or strategically taking discards that allow useful replacements. Target high-value cards (face cards, aces if high) for replacement. Favor Kings since they score zero points.\n\nColumn Control: Watch what cards have been revealed by opponents and in the discard pile. If you see two cards of the same rank, try to acquire the third to complete a zero-scoring column. Discard Management: Remember what's been played; avoid creating a discard pile full of cards that help opponents complete patterns. Position Awareness: Note which of your face-down cards are in the same column as high-value face-up cards, and prioritize replacing those face-down cards.\n\nEndgame Prediction: As the round nears completion (when someone has 8-9 face-up cards), calculate which opponent will reveal their final card and when. Block them by refusing to replace certain high-value cards if it helps you more. Strategic Discard Timing: Sometimes hold onto low-value discards to prevent opponents from accessing cards they need. Calculate Opponent Columns: Deduce what face-down cards opponents likely hold based on their play pattern, and avoid giving them opportunities to complete three-of-a-kind columns.",
    Player: "Early Game: Prioritize revealing your face-down cards quickly by drawing from the stock or strategically taking discards that allow useful replacements. Target high-value cards (face cards, aces if high) for replacement. Favor Kings since they score zero points.\n\nColumn Control: Watch what cards have been revealed by opponents and in the discard pile. If you see two cards of the same rank, try to acquire the third to complete a zero-scoring column. Discard Management: Remember what's been played; avoid creating a discard pile full of cards that help opponents complete patterns. Position Awareness: Note which of your face-down cards are in the same column as high-value face-up cards, and prioritize replacing those face-down cards.\n\nEndgame Prediction: As the round nears completion (when someone has 8-9 face-up cards), calculate which opponent will reveal their final card and when. Block them by refusing to replace certain high-value cards if it helps you more. Strategic Discard Timing: Sometimes hold onto low-value discards to prevent opponents from accessing cards they need. Calculate Opponent Columns: Deduce what face-down cards opponents likely hold based on their play pattern, and avoid giving them opportunities to complete three-of-a-kind columns.",
